    "science_context": "The nearest single star to the Sun (about 6 light-years) and the star with the fastest known motion across the sky: about 10.4″ per year, mostly northward (Gaia DR3), which is 1.7 SPHEREx pixels. Detector D3 (about 1.6–2.4 µm) at three epochs: August 2025 and March 2026 (data release QR2) and August 2026 (QR3). The marker shows the Gaia position propagated to each exposure, not a measured position. The star is bright enough to saturate its core (OVERFLOW flags), so a position needs a centroid and is best averaged over several frames: a one-year test averaging three and four frames measured 9.2″ per year against 10.39″ from Gaia. The epochs mix two data releases, so positions can be compared but brightness should not be."
